      <Value><![CDATA[# Designing for Every Screen

When I started styling this blog, I made a deliberate choice: one master CSS file for the entire site, not separate files per page.

## The Single CSS File Advantage

Having everything in one place (global.css) meant:
- Easier to maintain consistent spacing, sizing, and colors
- No duplicated styles
- Predictable cascade behavior
- Faster load times (one request, one cache)

## Responsive Breakpoints

I used mobile-first design with CSS Grid and Flexbox:
- Mobile: Single column, full-width
- Tablet: Two-column grid for blog posts
- Desktop: Three-column grid with sidebar potential

## CSS Variables for Themability

I could have hardcoded colors everywhere. Instead, I used CSS custom properties (variables) to define the AI Journey theme:

```
--primary: #2d1b69;
--secondary: #ff6b35;
--accent: #04d9ff;
```

Now if the client wants a different theme, I just change the variables.

## The Lesson

Responsive design isn't just about viewport widths. It's about creating a system that scales gracefully and remains maintainable as the site grows.]]></Value>
    </content>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[Creating a consistent visual identity across multiple page types is challenging. I discovered that a well-organized CSS architecture is the foundation of responsive design.]]></Value>
    </excerpt>
